  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 248 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: GOES-19 mid lvl water vapor imagery indicates strong \nsubsidence/dry air aloft, but plenty of moisture trapped below \ninversion is resulting in plenty of stratus clouds, especially in \nfavorable upslope areas. Secondary cold frnt and associated s/w \nenergy is approaching the Ottawa Valley this aftn, with additional \nspotty showers. Based on crnt radar imagery and favorable upslope \nflow under modest llvl caa have continued with chc pops acrs the \nnorthern Dacks and central/northern Greens thru 06z this evening. \nSounding data indicates the depth of moisture is only thru 850mb, so \nareal coverage and intensity of precip wl be light and mostly \nconfined to the trrn. Our CWA conts under caa tonight, with \nWhiteface Summit already down to 28F with windchill of 14F, so \nexpecting coolest values acrs the mid-upper elevations with warmest \nreadings in the valleys. Lows generally mid 20s to mid/upper 30s \novernight.\n\nOn Saturday a 1033mb high pres builds toward Binghamton, NY with \ncool northerly flow prevailing. Soundings show any lingering \nmoisture should dissipate, with just a few fair wx cumulus clouds \nexpected over the trrn. Progged 850mb temps range btwn -3C and -6C, \nwhich support highs from the mid/upper 20s summits to upper 40s \nwarmer valleys. SOundings indicate mixed layer winds of 10 to 15 \nknots, which wl result in sfc winds of 5 to 15 mph acrs most area. \nThese winds wl help mix slightly drier toward the sfc with min rh \nvalues in the 30 to 45% range. Lowest rhs wl be in the lower CT \nRiver Valley and near BTV,  with highest over the summits. \n\nSaturday night lows are tricky based on potential llvl moisture \ntrapped below strengthening subsidence inversion per latest NAM12 \nand NAM3KM sounding data. Meanwhile, the latest RRFS profiles \nindicate the potential for fog at SLK and other climo favored areas, \nbut feel reaching below cross over values maybe challenging. For now \nhave kept lows near NBM values, given the cloud potential and \nlingering bl winds of 4 to 8 knots. Lows generally mid 20s to mid \n30s. \n\nSunday and Monday are quiet with sfc high pres shifting off the Mid \nAtlantic coast and developing llvl waa. This wl produce another \nwindow of above normal temps for early next week with highs well \ninto the 50s on Sunday and upper 50s to mid 60s by Monday. The dry \nconditions prevail, except a few sprinkles are possible Monday \nmorning associated with warm front over northern NY. Moisture and \ndynamics are limited, so potential is small attm.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Still some model disagreement with how our next weather \nmaker will play out, but at this time next rainfall to reach our \nregion will arrive later next week. GFS indicates a low pressure \nsystem that passes much too far north of our region to be too \nimpactful, and a weak front crosses the area Wednesday night into \nThursday. ECMWF shows a low pressure system passing much closer to \nour area which has potential for some better moisture and also \nprecipitation chances, and has a bit of a slower solution with bulk \nof precipitation arriving on Friday. A lot of uncertainty exists \nthis far out, but details will become more clear as we approach the \nsecond half of next week.",
